er heard of it said I don't  know or refuse to answer the question  into one category that we're calling the  unaware of climate change and when we  calculate that out across the world we  basically find that four out of ten  people around the world  representing about 1.9 billion people  have never heard of this issue let me  say that again about four out of ten  people around the world have never heard  of this issue that doesn't mean they  haven't seen the impacts in their own  lives but they don't have climate change  or global warming as a conceptual  framework to make sense of the changes  or to understand where those changes may  be going and this is despite decades of  increasing in more reliable science this  is despite years of media reporting and  the fact that climate change is now at  the very highest level of the the  world's political agenda even despite  that we still see very large numbers of  people around the world who are unaware  of this  we've now mapped that on the map that  you should see on your screen and what  you see there are the countries in blue  are those countries where there's very  high awareness where people generally do  and know about it and in country in  countries that are colored in red and  orange by contrast or the countries  where you see very high levels of  unawareness and what you very quickly  can see here is there's a very stark  north-south divide countries in North  America Europe Australia Japan very high  levels of public awareness in central  South America Africa and South Asia very  high levels of people who are unaware of  this issue next slide please  so here we looked at some of the  countries where there's the largest  number of people who are unaware of this  issue and we divided them into these  four quadrants you see along the left or  the y-axis the percent of the population  in each of these countries that's  unaware and then along the bottom axis  or the x axis we see the millions of  people that represents in each of these  countries so looking at the lower left  we see that Mexico and Brazil are doing  reasonably well compared to most of  these others Brazil about 22% Mexico  about 36 37% are unaware about the issue  but let's now contrast that with China  lower right-hand corner  China's about the same as Mexico but  because China is such an enormous  country population wise we're talking  about over 400 million people who have  never heard of this issue within China  alone and when we look just above that  to India it  even more Stark 64 percent of Indians  have never heard of this issue and that  represents about 485 million people now  remember you know that's a huge numbers  of people that's far larger than the  entire population of say a country like  the United States several United States  in fact and these are also countries  that are now crucial players in the  negotiations both in the mitigation side  and adaptation and I'll come back to  that theme in the upper left hand corner  we see those countries where there are  particularly high percentages who have  never heard of the issue again and I'd  like to take a closer look in particular  at Bangladesh next slide please  number five this is the unaware in  Bangladesh now Bangladesh has become  kind of an international poster child  many people think of Bangladesh when  they think about the impacts of climate  change and on the left you see a picture  of an analysis produced by the  University of Dhaka which tries to  estimate what a 1.5 meter sea-level rise  might look like and that's within  scientific projections as you can see  they think that's going to impact about  18 million people submerge about 22  thousand square kilometers of land  clearly that's going to have enormous  impacts not just on those people but  those people have to go somewhere which  will have of course ripple effects  throughout the entire region as they  migrate both within country and to other  countries but it's also important to  recognize that Bangladesh is also  vulnerable in another important ways and  that's through fresh water is where the  Brahmaputra and the Ganges rivers both  glacier-fed rivers that stem in the  Himalaya and we now know that glaciers  in the hem a liar are melting away  faster than almost anywhere else in the  world  so this is the country that's clearly  quite vulnerable well how aware of this  are they on the right we've looked at  the data by region and we see that  anywhere from fifty eight to seventy  four percent of Bangladeshis have never  heard of this issue  now again vulnerability is not simply a  function of physical changes in the  environment or the human infrastructure  that's at risk it's also about what's in  people's heads and if you don't know  you're vulnerable you are more  vulnerable  because it makes it impossible for you  to start taking the protective actions  now to deal with the impacts that are  already happening and going to happen in  the future next slide please  this is slide number 6 and this is just  to represent what we see of course  around the world why are people unaware  well largely that's got a lot to do with  basic levels of education here we look  at those Bangladeshis who have only an  elementary school education or less and  we find that about 97% of those people  have never heard of this issue by  contrast those Bangladeshis who have had  a college education only about 2% of  them have never heard of the issue so  again  climate change is a crucial issue but it  cannot be separated from the larger  challenge of sustainable development  around the world knowledge and  understanding and ability to both  mitigate and adapt to climate change  cannot be separated from the broader  needs of human development including  education of people all over the world  next slide please  slide number 7 ok so we then wanted to  look all right well of the 60% of people  around the world who have heard of  climate change do they understand the  causes so this question asks temperature  rises part of global warming or climate  change do you think rising temperatures  are a result of human activities or  result of natural causes and here  fortunately we see some good results 58  percent of people around the world do  believe that it's human activities that  are primarily responsible about 25  percent say it's natural 14 percent  volunteer that it's both and only 3  percent of those who've heard of climate  change say that they don't know well  let's take a closer look however at the  distribution of those people who still  believe that is naturally caused next  slide please  slide number 8 so in this map we're  mapping where the largest numbers of  people are who but still believe that  climate change is naturally caused and  you can see here the countries in red  and in orange are the ones where there  are the highest proportions of those  people know in particular the United  States and India next slide please  slide number 9 so here we've taken the  12 major emitters what we might call the  dirty does  and we've tried to see well how does  knowledge about the causes of climate  change fall out across these countries  and ways to here in the lower-left is  that some countries like Japan are doing  quite well only about 6% of Japanese  believe that climate change is naturally  caused Malaysia Brazil Mexico Germany  all do quite well on that but as we keep  working our way up we find that Russia  the UK and of course the United States  there are still very significant  proportions of the population in the US  it's about 37 percent who believe that  climate change is natural not a human  cause problem but when we look over  across at the other two countries China  in particular is doing much better on  this interestingly than the United  States a far lower percentage of their  population believes climate change is  natural but again China is an enormous  country and that still works out to  abou